Here is the combination circuit you will meet on every job. A source feeds a run of conductors; at the far end, several loads hang in parallel. The conductor resistance is in **series** with the parallel **group**, so the conductors take their share of the voltage first and the loads get the remainder.

The lower the combined resistance of the loads (the more of them, or the bigger they are), the more current flows, the more voltage the conductors drop, and the less the loads receive. This "loading effect" is why:

- a heater at the end of a long circuit runs cooler than its rating;
- lights dim when a motor starts on the same circuit (the motor's inrush briefly drops the conductor voltage for everyone);
- voltage must be measured **at the load, under load** to judge a voltage-drop complaint.

The arithmetic is the same reduce-and-redraw: combine the loads into one equivalent, add the conductor resistance, find total current, then compute the conductor drop and subtract it from the source.
